# SPIKE Prime over Web Serial

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino_web_serial, "~> 0.1.0"},
  {:ex_spike, "~> 0.1.0"}
])
```

## About

This notebook talks to a LEGO® SPIKE™ Prime hub over a USB cable.
[KinoWebSerial](https://github.com/ErikMejerHansen/kino_web_serial)
carries the bytes over your browser's Web Serial API, and
[ExSPIKE](https://github.com/ErikMejerHansen/ex_spike) turns them into
messages.

The hub speaks the same protocol over USB as over Bluetooth: COBS
framed messages on a serial port at 115200 baud
([LEGO/spike-prime-docs#11](https://github.com/LEGO/spike-prime-docs/issues/11)).

You need a Chromium based browser (Chrome, Edge) and a hub running the
SPIKE App 3 firmware. Connect the hub to your computer with a USB cable
and turn it on. Close the SPIKE App first, only one program can use the
port at a time.

> ExSPIKE and KinoWebSerial are not affiliated with, sponsored,
> authorized or endorsed by The LEGO Group. LEGO® and SPIKE™ are
> trademarks of The LEGO Group.

## Connect

Evaluate the Smart Cell below, click **Connect** and pick your hub. The
cell only offers LEGO hubs (USB vendor `0694`, product `0009`).

The monitor shows the raw frames going back and forth, in hex. Click
**Send** to send the prefilled `00 00 02`: an encoded `InfoRequest`.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoWebSerial.SmartCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
hub_port = KinoWebSerial.port("serial-spike-hub")
```

## A small helper

`Hub.request/3` sends an ExSPIKE message and waits for the reply.

A serial port is a byte stream: a chunk of data can hold part of a
frame, or several frames. ExSPIKE is stateless, so the helper keeps the
bytes of an unfinished frame and hands them back to
`ExSPIKE.decode_stream/1` with the next chunk.

```elixir
defmodule Hub do
  @doc "Sends a message (a struct or raw bytes) and waits for a reply of the given type."
  def request(port, message, reply_type, timeout \\ 5_000) do
    # Subscribe first, so the reply can't arrive before we listen
    :ok = KinoWebSerial.subscribe(port)

    try do
      :ok = KinoWebSerial.write(port, ExSPIKE.encode(message))
      await(port, reply_type, <<>>, timeout)
    after
      KinoWebSerial.unsubscribe(port)
      flush(port)
    end
  end

  defp await(port, reply_type, buffer, timeout) do
    receive do
      {:kino_web_serial, :data, ^port, bytes} ->
        {results, rest} = ExSPIKE.decode_stream(buffer <> bytes)

        case Enum.find(results, &match?({:ok, %{__struct__: ^reply_type}}, &1)) do
          {:ok, reply} -> {:ok, reply}
          nil -> await(port, reply_type, rest, timeout)
        end

      {:kino_web_serial, :disconnected, ^port} ->
        {:error, :disconnected}
    after
      timeout -> {:error, :timeout}
    end
  end

  defp flush(port) do
    receive do
      {:kino_web_serial, :data, ^port, _bytes} -> flush(port)
    after
      0 -> :ok
    end
  end
end

:ok = KinoWebSerial.await_connected(hub_port)
```

## Ask the hub about itself

Always send an `InfoRequest` first. The reply tells us, among other
things, the largest chunk for file transfers.

```elixir
alias ExSPIKE.{Message, Messages}

{:ok, info} = Hub.request(hub_port, Messages.info_request(), Message.InfoResponse)
```

```elixir
{:ok, %Message.GetHubNameResponse{name: name}} =
  Hub.request(hub_port, Messages.get_hub_name(), Message.GetHubNameResponse)

name
```

Messages can also be written as raw bytes. `0x1A` is a `DeviceUuidRequest`:

```elixir
{:ok, %Message.DeviceUuidResponse{uuid: uuid}} =
  Hub.request(hub_port, <<0x1A>>, Message.DeviceUuidResponse)

Base.encode16(uuid)
```

## Watch what the hub sends

This cell listens to everything the hub sends, in the background. Console
output from programs appears on the left, and sensor and motor readings
on the right.

```elixir
console = Kino.Frame.new()
sensors = Kino.Frame.new()

hub_port
|> KinoWebSerial.stream()
|> Stream.transform(<<>>, fn bytes, buffer ->
  {results, rest} = ExSPIKE.decode_stream(buffer <> bytes)
  {for({:ok, message} <- results, do: message), rest}
end)
|> Kino.listen(fn
  %Message.ConsoleNotification{text: text} ->
    Kino.Frame.append(console, Kino.Text.new(text))

  %Message.DeviceNotification{messages: devices} ->
    Kino.Frame.render(sensors, Kino.Tree.new(devices))

  _other ->
    :ok
end)

Kino.Layout.grid([console, sensors], columns: 2, boxed: true)
```

Ask the hub to send its sensor and motor readings twice a second. Plug in
a motor or sensor and watch the readings above change.

```elixir
Hub.request(hub_port, Messages.device_notifications(500), Message.DeviceNotificationResponse)
```

## Upload and run a program

This uploads a MicroPython program to a slot and starts it. Its `print`
output shows up in the console above.

> This replaces the program stored in the slot.

```elixir
slot = 0

program = """
import runloop
from hub import light_matrix

print("Hello from Elixir")

async def main():
    await light_matrix.write("Hi From Elixir!")

runloop.run(main())
"""

# The slot may already be empty, so any reply is fine here
{:ok, _} = Hub.request(hub_port, Messages.clear_slot(slot), Message.ClearSlotResponse)

upload = Messages.start_file_upload("program.py", slot, ExSPIKE.CRC.crc32(program))

{:ok, %{status: :acknowledged}} =
  Hub.request(hub_port, upload, Message.StartFileUploadResponse)

for chunk <- Messages.transfer_chunks(program, info.max_chunk_size) do
  {:ok, %{status: :acknowledged}} =
    Hub.request(hub_port, chunk, Message.TransferChunkResponse)
end

Hub.request(hub_port, Messages.start_program(slot), Message.ProgramFlowResponse)
```

Stop the program:

```elixir
Hub.request(hub_port, Messages.stop_program(slot), Message.ProgramFlowResponse)
```

## Clean up

Turn off the sensor readings, then close the port so other programs,
like the SPIKE App, can use it.

```elixir
Hub.request(hub_port, Messages.device_notifications(0), Message.DeviceNotificationResponse)
KinoWebSerial.disconnect(hub_port)
```
